.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
layer keeps a machine-independent description of how capable each CPU is,
so that a scheduler can place threads on heterogeneous
.Pq Dq hybrid
processors without knowing how that information was obtained.
It sits between
.Em providers ,
machine-dependent code or drivers that discover the information, and
.Em consumers ,
such as the
.Xr sched_mic 4
scheduler, that act on it.
It is modelled on the
.Xr hmp 4
framework proposed for
.Fx
so that the two can converge.
.Pp
Four values are kept for every CPU:
.Bl -tag -width "capacity" -offset indent
.It class
One of
.Dv CPU_CLASS_PERF
.Pq 1 ,
.Dv CPU_CLASS_EFF
.Pq 2
or
.Dv CPU_CLASS_LP
.Pq 4
from
.In sys/smp.h :
a performance core, an efficiency core, or an Intel low-power E-core.
The default is
.Dv CPU_CLASS_PERF .
.It capacity
The static relative performance of the CPU on a scale of 0 to
.Dv CPUCAP_CAPACITY_MAX
.Pq 1024 ,
the same scale
.Xr hmp 4
uses.
The default is 1024.
.It perf, eff
Dynamic performance and energy-efficiency scores on a scale of 0 to
.Dv CPUCAP_SCORE_MAX
.Pq 255 ,
the scale the Intel Hardware Feedback Interface reports natively.
Both default to 255 and are only meaningful while
.Fn cpucap_has_scores
returns true.
.El
.Pp
Two platform hints accompany them:
.Fn cpucap_compute_class
names the class that holds the platform's compute cores
.Po
.Dv CPU_CLASS_PERF
on Intel,
.Dv CPU_CLASS_EFF
on AMD parts whose dies have different last-level cache sizes
.Pc
and
.Fn cpucap_prefer_compute
says whether that class should be preferred by default.
.Pp
Without any provider every CPU is a performance core of capacity 1024,
so a consumer behaves as it would on a homogeneous machine.
.Ss Providers
A provider is described by a
.Vt struct cpucap_provider :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct cpucap_provider {
	const char	*cp_name;
	int		cp_kind;	/* CPUCAP_PROVIDER_* */
	int		cp_priority;	/* higher wins */
	int		(*cp_probe)(void);
	int		(*cp_init)(void);
	/* ... owned by the layer */
};
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Dv CPUCAP_PROVIDER_STATIC
provider supplies class, capacity and the platform hints once, from
.Fa cp_init .
A
.Dv CPUCAP_PROVIDER_DYNAMIC
provider supplies scores from
.Fa cp_init
and may update them later from thread or taskqueue context.
At most one provider of each kind is active: the one with the highest
.Fa cp_priority
whose
.Fa cp_probe
returns zero and whose
.Fa cp_init
then returns zero.
Selection runs once at boot, at
.Dv SI_SUB_SMP ,
.Dv SI_ORDER_MIDDLE ,
after the application processors have started;
.Fn CPUCAP_PROVIDER_DECLARE
registers a statically defined provider from a
.Xr SYSINIT 9
that runs just before that.
A provider registered later, by a device driver or a loadable module, is
probed on registration and replaces a lower-priority active provider of its
kind.
.Fn cpucap_provider_register
returns
.Er EINVAL
for a malformed provider and
.Er EEXIST
for one already registered.
.Fn cpucap_provider_unregister
removes a provider, resets the values it supplied to their defaults and
falls back to the next best provider of its kind, if any.
.Pp
Before a static provider's
.Fa cp_init
runs, the layer resets every class and capacity to the default; the routine
must therefore set every CPU it classifies.
Before a dynamic provider's
.Fa cp_init
runs, the scores are reset.
.Pp
Providers write values with
.Fn cpucap_set_class ,
.Fn cpucap_set_capacity ,
.Fn cpucap_set_scores
and
.Fn cpucap_set_compute_class .
Each stores one naturally aligned value and ignores out-of-range CPUs and
values.
A provider writes all the CPUs of a batch, then calls
.Fn cpucap_commit
once.
That increments the generation counter and, after boot, queues one
notification on
.Va taskqueue_thread ;
commits that arrive before the notification runs are coalesced.
.Fn cpucap_commit
may be called from any context that may acquire a default
.Xr mutex 9 ,
so from a thread, an interrupt thread or a taskqueue, but not from an
interrupt filter.
During boot-time selection consumers are instead called synchronously.
.Pp
The class of a CPU may be overridden by the
.Va kern.cpucap.force_classes
tunable, described below; the override is applied by the layer and a
provider's
.Fn cpucap_set_class
for such a CPU is ignored.
.Ss Consumers
A consumer is described by a
.Vt struct cpucap_consumer :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct cpucap_consumer {
	const char	*cc_name;
	void		(*cc_update)(void *arg, int changed);
	void		*cc_arg;
	/* ... owned by the layer */
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Fa cc_update
runs in thread context after a commit, with
.Fa changed
set to the union of
.Dv CPUCAP_CHANGED_STATIC
.Pq class, capacity or a hint changed
and
.Dv CPUCAP_CHANGED_SCORES
over every commit since the previous notification.
It is called with the layer's lock held, so it must not sleep and must not
register or unregister providers or consumers.
.Fn cpucap_consumer_register
calls
.Fa cc_update
immediately if any commit has already happened, so a consumer need not
order its own initialization against the providers.
.Pp
The intended use is for a consumer to derive whatever precomputed per-CPU
tables its hot paths need inside
.Fa cc_update
and publish them with atomic stores; the hot paths then never call into
.Nm .
.Ss Reading values
.Fn cpucap_class ,
.Fn cpucap_capacity ,
.Fn cpucap_perf
and
.Fn cpucap_eff
return the current value for a CPU, or the default for a CPU outside
.Bq 0, MAXCPU ) .
Each is a single relaxed atomic load of a naturally aligned value, so a
caller racing a provider sees either the old or the new value, never a torn
one, and never blocks.
.Fn cpucap_generation
returns the number of commits so far.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va kern.cpucap.class , kern.cpucap.capacity , kern.cpucap.perf , kern.cpucap.eff
Every present CPU's value as
.Dq Va cpuid : Ns Va value
pairs.
.It Va kern.cpucap.cpu.N.{class,capacity,perf,eff}
The same values, one node per CPU.
.It Va kern.cpucap.compute_class , kern.cpucap.prefer_compute
The platform hints.
.It Va kern.cpucap.has_scores
Whether a dynamic provider is active.
.It Va kern.cpucap.generation
The number of commits so far.
.It Va kern.cpucap.providers
Every registered provider as
.Dq Ar name Ns ( Ns Ar kind , priority , state ) ,
where state is one of
.Li registered , absent , failed , probed
or
.Li active .
.It Va kern.cpucap.force_classes
A boot-time tunable for testing.
When set to a comma-separated list of classes such as
.Dq Li 1,1,1,1,2,2,2,2,4,4
the class of CPUs 0, 1, ... is replaced by the listed values
(1 performance, 2 efficiency, 4 low-power), whatever any provider says;
CPUs beyond the end of the list keep their provided class.
A malformed list is ignored with a console message.
.Va kern.sched.mic.force_classes
is accepted as an alias for existing configurations.
.It Va kern.cpucap.detect_lpe
A boot-time tunable of the x86 CPUID provider: when non-zero, the default,
Intel small cores without a last-level cache are classified as low-power
.Pq class 4
rather than efficiency
.Pq class 2
cores.
.Va kern.sched.detect_lpe
is accepted as an alias.
.El
